VERILOG - COMPLETE REFERENCE GUIDE
==================================

Verilog is a Hardware Description Language (HDL) used to model electronic systems. This guide covers commonly used Verilog constructs, data types, tasks, functions, parameters, and system tasks.


1. DATA TYPES & VARIABLES
=========================

1.1 NETS (WIRES)
----------------
Syntax: wire [range] name;
Explanation: Represents physical connections between structural elements. Wires do not store values; they must be driven continuously by an assign statement or connected to a module output.
Example:
  wire a;                  // 1-bit wire
  wire [7:0] bus;          // 8-bit wire bus
  assign bus = 8'hA5;      // Driving a wire


1.2 REGISTERS (REG)
-------------------
Syntax: reg [range] name;
Explanation: Represents a data storage element. Unlike wires, registers hold their value until a new value is assigned to them in a procedural block (always or initial block).
Example:
  reg q;                   // 1-bit register
  reg [31:0] data;         // 32-bit register
  
  always @(posedge clk) begin
      q <= d;              // Assigning value to a reg
  end


1.3 OTHER DATA TYPES
--------------------
* integer: 32-bit signed data type, commonly used for loop counters.
* real: 64-bit double-precision floating-point number.
* time: 64-bit unsigned variable used to store simulation time.
Example:
  integer i;
  real pi = 3.14159;
  time current_time;


1.4 VECTORS & ARRAYS
--------------------
* Vectors: Multi-bit wire or reg.
* Arrays: Multiple instances of scalar or vector variables (unpacked).
Example:
  reg [7:0] mem [0:255];   // 256 bytes of memory (Array of 8-bit registers)
  wire [3:0] flags [0:7];  // Array of eight 4-bit wire vectors


2. MODULES & PORTS
==================

2.1 MODULE DEFINITION
---------------------
Modules are the building blocks of Verilog designs.
Example:
  module adder (
      input  wire [31:0] a,
      input  wire [31:0] b,
      output reg  [31:0] sum
  );
      always @(*) begin
          sum = a + b;
      end
  endmodule


3. PARAMETERS & CONSTANTS
=========================

3.1 PARAMETER (Run-time Overridable)
------------------------------------
Syntax: parameter NAME = VALUE;
Explanation: Defines constants that can be overridden at the time of module instantiation. Useful for configuring bit-widths, memory depths, or speeds.
Example:
  module generic_register #(
      parameter WIDTH = 8
  ) (
      input  wire clk,
      input  wire [WIDTH-1:0] d,
      output reg  [WIDTH-1:0] q
  );
      always @(posedge clk) begin
          q <= d;
      end
  endmodule

// Instantiation with parameter override:
  generic_register #(.WIDTH(16)) reg16 ( .clk(clk), .d(data_in), .q(data_out) );


3.2 LOCALPARAM (Compile-time Constant)
--------------------------------------
Syntax: localparam NAME = VALUE;
Explanation: Defines a local constant that cannot be modified directly during instantiation. Used for internal state codes, derived constants, or fixed parameters.
Example:
  localparam IDLE = 2'b00;
  localparam READ = 2'b01;
  localparam WRITE = 2'b10;


4. PROCEDURAL BLOCKS
====================

4.1 ALWAYS BLOCK
----------------
Executes repeatedly throughout simulation.
* Combinational Logic (always @(*)): Automatically triggers when any input changes.
* Sequential Logic (always @(posedge clk)): Triggers on specific clock edges.
Example:
  // Combinational
  always @(*) begin
      y = a & b;
  end

  // Sequential
  always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
      if (!rst_n)
          q <= 1'b0;
      else
          q <= d;
  end


4.2 INITIAL BLOCK
-----------------
Executes only once at the beginning of simulation. Primarily used in testbenches for initialization and stimulus generation.
Example:
  initial begin
      clk = 0;
      rst_n = 0;
      #20 rst_n = 1;       // Release reset after 20 time units
  end


5. OPERATORS
============

5.1 ARITHMETIC OPERATORS
------------------------
* `+` (Add), `-` (Subtract), `*` (Multiply), `/` (Divide), `%` (Modulus), `**` (Exponentiation)

5.2 LOGICAL OPERATORS (Returns 1-bit 0 or 1)
--------------------------------------------
* `&&` (Logical AND), `||` (Logical OR), `!` (Logical NOT)

5.3 BITWISE OPERATORS (Performs bit-by-bit operations)
------------------------------------------------------
* `&` (AND), `|` (OR), `^` (XOR), `~` (NOT), `~^` or `^~` (XNOR)

5.4 REDUCTION OPERATORS (Combines vector bits into a single bit)
----------------------------------------------------------------
* `&` (Reduction AND), `|` (Reduction OR), `^` (Reduction XOR), `~&` (Reduction NAND)
Example: `y = &4'b1111;` // y is 1

5.5 CONCATENATION & REPLICATION
-------------------------------
* Concatenation `{a, b}`: Joins multiple vectors together.
* Replication `{N{a}}`: Repeats vector `a` `N` times.
Example:
  wire [7:0] byte_out;
  assign byte_out = {4'b1010, 4'b0101}; // 8'b10100101
  
  wire [15:0] sign_extended;
  assign sign_extended = {{8{data_in[7]}}, data_in}; // Sign-extends 8-bit to 16-bit


6. FUNCTIONS & TASKS
====================

6.1 FUNCTIONS
-------------
Explanation: Used to perform combinational calculations. They cannot contain time-controlling statements (like `#`, `@`, `wait`), cannot call tasks, and must return a single value.
Syntax:
  function [range] name;
      input_declarations;
      begin
          statements;
          name = return_value; // Assign return value to function name
      end
  endfunction

Example:
  function [7:0] absolute;
      input signed [7:0] val;
      begin
          if (val < 0)
              absolute = -val;
          else
              absolute = val;
      end
  endfunction


6.2 TASKS
---------
Explanation: Can represent both combinational and sequential logic. Tasks can contain delay statements, can have input, output, and inout arguments, and do not return a value directly.
Syntax:
  task name;
      input/output_declarations;
      begin
          statements;
      end
  endtask

Example:
  task write_mem;
      input [7:0] addr;
      input [31:0] data;
      begin
          mem_write_en = 1;
          mem_addr = addr;
          mem_data = data;
          #10;               // Time delay allowed in task
          mem_write_en = 0;
      end
  endtask


7. SYSTEM TASKS & FUNCTIONS
===========================

Commonly used system tasks during simulation:
* `$display`: Prints formatted text to the console (with newline).
* `$write`: Prints formatted text to the console (without newline).
* `$monitor`: Prints variables automatically whenever their values change.
* `$time`: Returns current simulation time as a 64-bit integer.
* `$finish`: Terminates the simulation.
* `$stop`: Pauses the simulation.
* `$random`: Generates a random signed 32-bit integer.

Example:
  $display("Time = %0d, ALU Result = %d", $time, result);


8. COMPILER DIRECTIVES
======================

* `` `define ``: Creates a text macro.
* `` `undef ``: Undefines a text macro.
* `` `include ``: Includes another file at compile time.
* `` `timescale ``: Specifies reference time unit / time precision.
Example:
  `timescale 1ns / 1ps   // Time unit = 1ns, Precision = 1ps
  `define ADDR_WIDTH 32
